  "market_context": "During a downtrend on EURUSD 15-minute chart, price formed a new lower low near 1.20273–1.20550 range, while RSI (period 14, bands adjusted to 50) formed a higher low, signaling weakening bearish momentum at the bottom of the range.",
  "key_observation": "Price made a lower low while RSI made a higher low — a classic bullish regular divergence — occurring at the bottom of a price range, preceding a reversal upward.",
  "analysis_steps": [
    "Step 1: The analyst configured RSI with upper/lower bands set to 50 (not 70/30) to treat it as a momentum oscillator, coloring the line yellow for visual consistency with SMMA channels.",
    "Step 2: On the EURUSD 15-minute chart, the analyst identified a recent swing low where price printed a lower low (e.g., ~1.20273), while the corresponding RSI value was higher than the prior low (e.g., RSI ~27.74 vs prior ~25), forming a bullish regular divergence.",
    "Step 3: The analyst emphasized that this divergence occurred at the bottom of a range and consistently precedes reversals, contrasting it with premature entries during ongoing trends where divergence appears but momentum hasn’t yet shifted."
  ],
  "outcome": null,
  "lessons": "Bullish RSI divergences at market bottoms are high-probability reversal signals when price makes a lower low but RSI makes a higher low—especially when confirmed by structure shift; avoid entering before momentum actually changes hands.",
